<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">March of Turin</h1><p class="st-meta">941 CE – 1091 CE (approx.) · capital Turin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">March of the Arduinici in Piedmont whose heiress Adelaide of Susa brought its lands to the house of Savoy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 941 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-arles">Hugh of Arles</a> or his successor reorganised the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arduin-glaber">Arduin Glaber</a> control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> valley. Arduin drove out Arab raiders based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraxinetum">Fraxinetum</a> from the passes, and his family, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arduinici">Arduinici</a>, held the march for 150 years.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The margraves controlled the routes over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-cenis">Mont Cenis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montgenevre-pass">Mont Genèvre</a> passes, a source of wealth and strategic weight. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulric-manfred-ii">Ulric Manfred II</a> (died 1034) extended the march and founded monasteries. His daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-susa">Adelaide of Susa</a> ruled for over half a century, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-count-of-savoy">Otto of Savoy</a>, and was one of the most powerful figures in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the mother-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Henry IV</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-susa">Adelaide</a> died in 1091, the march broke up among her heirs, local lords and the growing commune of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>. Her Savoyard descendants eventually inherited Turin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, the foundation of the later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-savoy">Savoy</a> state.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/march-of-turin.jpg" alt="March of Turin" width="320" height="278"><figcaption>Image: Dodolulupepe · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marches_of_the_Kingdom_of_Italy_in_the_900s.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>941 CE – 1091 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Turin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Arduin Glaber, Ulric Manfred II, Adelaide of Susa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Arduinic March, Margraviate of Turin, March of Susa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1016&amp;focus=march-of-turin" aria-label="March of Turin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4863 586 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where March of Turin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5183 842L5218 839M5183 842L5140 827"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5218 839h0M5140 827h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5183 842h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5183 842h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Turin. <a href="/atlas?year=1016&amp;focus=march-of-turin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
